            <div type="translation">
                <p><!--translation(s) - add @xml:lang for 'en' or 'it' to div--></p>
            </div>
            <div type="commentary">
                <p>Orsi declined to speculate on the nature of the text in his original publication. Kaibel suggested that the fragment was part of a funerary verse epigram, a view which Manganaro merely repeated without reviewing the text in 1994. In 1999 instead, he suggested that this might be a fragment of an honorific text for a teacher of the youth of the gymnasium, and although his restorations are excessively speculative, the core points that we would seem to have the words καιρὸν, a form of the participle παιδευόμενος (the sequence of letters can hardly be restored as anything else), and a reference to ἀρετή, very strongly favour the suggestion. The vacats after lines 1 and 5 encourage the view that the right margin of the stone is preserved. The ΤΩΝ at the start of line 1 are perhaps more likely to be the end of a genitive plural, rather than the article, given the vacat. The survival of a high quality painted text from the Hellenistic period is unusual, but especially given the relatively poor quality of the local stone for epigraphic carving, perhaps should not be surprising.</p>
            </div>
